#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Helix Boot on a Mac: keep a Helix Boot stick up to date.

    mac/helix-mac                  update the stick that's plugged in (it asks first)
    mac/helix-mac update --from helix-boot-2026-10-04.zip     from a pack, no downloads
    mac/helix-mac check            read the stick back: any damaged or missing files?
    mac/helix-mac look --theme midnight      change its look (the options of `helix theme`)
    mac/helix-mac list             the Helix Boot / Ventoy sticks it can see
    mac/helix-mac helix …          the engine itself: helix list, check, fetch, pack …
    mac/helix-mac install --disk disk4       EXPERIMENTAL: erase a USB disk and make a new stick

Ventoy has no installer for macOS, so `install` writes Ventoy's layout onto the disk itself, the
way Ventoy's own installer does on Linux. CI boots a disk made that way, but it has not been
tried on many real sticks: if it doesn't boot, make the stick once on a PC (install.sh or
HelixBoot.exe) and keep it current from here. Nothing here boots a Mac; the stick's Mac folder
holds tools to copy onto one. Needs Python 3.11 or newer (python.org, or `brew install python`).

Your local.toml and byo/ folder are the ones beside the `helix` script, as on Linux; with the
one-file download (HelixBoot-mac), the ones beside that file.
"""
